('hampi-in-three-days','Hampi in three days, without rushing','A slow route through the boulders: temples at dawn, river crossings at noon, and the best sunset in Karnataka.','Most visitors try to see Hampi in a day and leave with a camera full of boulders and a blur of temples. Three days is the honest minimum.

Day one belongs to the south bank. Start at Virupaksha before the tour buses arrive, walk the bazaar, then climb Matanga Hill for sunrise if you can manage it. The afternoon is for the Vittala complex and the stone chariot, which deserves an hour rather than ten minutes.

Day two, cross the river to Anegundi. A coracle costs almost nothing and the mood changes completely: banana fields, a small palace, and a Hanuman temple on the hill. Cycle if you can, and eat a long lunch.

Keep day three for the royal enclosure and the museum, then drive to sunrise point above the valley. The best photographs in Hampi are not of the temples — they are of the light moving across the boulders at the end of the day.

Carry water, wear shoes you can climb in, and remember that many of these temples are still in use.','/images/hampi.jpg',1,'itinerary',6,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('coorg-without-a-car','Coorg without a car','How to use buses, homestays and estate walks to see the coffee country slowly.','Coorg looks like a driving destination, and in many ways it is. But the district buses are reliable, cheap and full of conversation, and a well-chosen homestay will pick you up from Madikeri.

Base yourself near Madikeri for two nights. Estate walks start at the doorstep, and the town has the bus stand you need for everything else. Abbey Falls is a short auto ride; Raja''s Seat is walkable from the centre.

For the second half, move to a plantation homestay near Kushalnagar. The host will know which waterfall is actually flowing, which coffee factory is running that week, and where to eat Kodava food without a menu.

The one thing worth hiring a jeep for is Mandalpatti. The track is rough, and a local driver will get you up and back before the afternoon rain.','/images/coorg.jpg',3,'field-notes',5,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('karnataka-hidden-gems','Five Karnataka places that are not on the usual list','Chikmagalur''s waterfall estates, Gokarna''s third beach, and three more reasons to leave the highway.','Karnataka rewards anyone willing to take one more turn off the main road. Here are five places we keep returning to.

Chikmagalur''s Hebbe Falls sits inside a coffee estate and requires a shared jeep and a forest walk. The reward is a two-stage waterfall with almost no one around.

Gokarna''s Paradise Beach is a twenty-minute cliff walk from Om Beach and has no buildings at all. Check the tide before you go.

Anegundi, across the river from Hampi, is a working village with a palace ruin and a coracle crossing. Stay a night and you will have the boulders to yourself at sunrise.

Kabini''s buffer zone is where the wildlife actually is. Book a forest department safari rather than a resort drive if you want the quieter ranges.

Mawphlang in Meghalaya is outside Karnataka, but it belongs on any hidden-gems list: a sacred grove protected by community ritual for centuries.','/images/munnar.jpg',4,'hidden-gems',5,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('first-timers-rajasthan','A first-timer''s Rajasthan','Jaipur, Udaipur and the desert, sequenced so the trip keeps getting better.','Rajasthan can overwhelm a first visit. The trick is sequence: start with the busiest city, end with the quietest landscape.

Begin in Jaipur for three nights. Amber Fort at opening time, the bazaars in the afternoon, and one evening on a rooftop inside the old walls. Buy the composite monument ticket and use it properly.

Take the train to Udaipur. Two nights is enough for the City Palace, a lake boat and a slow dinner. Do not over-schedule Udaipur; the point is the light on the water.

End in Jaisalmer, three nights including one in the desert. The fort is a living neighbourhood, so stay inside it if you can. A camel ride is optional; the dunes at sunset are not.

October to March is the comfortable window. In summer, shift the whole plan to the early mornings and accept that afternoons belong to the pool.','/images/jaipur.jpg',10,'itinerary',7,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('kerala-backwaters-guide','How to do the Kerala backwaters properly','Houseboat, shikara or kayak? A practical guide to Alleppey and the Kuttanad canals.','The backwaters are not one experience. A houseboat is the most famous, and an overnight cruise is genuinely lovely — but it is also the least flexible way to see the canals.

If you have one day, take a shikara through the smaller canals. They are narrow enough to reach villages and paddy fields that houseboats cannot enter, and the pace is closer to the actual life of Kuttanad.

If you have two days, spend one night on a houseboat and one in a village homestay. The homestay will feed you better and teach you more.

Kayaking is best in the early morning, before the tourist boats start. Several operators in Alleppey run guided sunrise paddles with a support boat.

Whatever you choose, book a licensed operator, ask about the crew, and avoid the single-use plastic that still turns up along the canals.','/images/kerala.jpg',8,'field-notes',5,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('ladakh-acclimatisation','Ladakh, acclimatised','Why the first two days in Leh decide the rest of your trip.','Ladakh is not difficult, but it is high. Leh sits at 3,500 metres and Pangong at 4,350, and the body needs time to adjust. The travellers who have a bad trip are usually the ones who drove to Nubra on day two.

Spend two full days in Leh doing very little. Walk the old town, drink water, skip alcohol, and sleep early. If you feel a headache, rest rather than push on.

Day three can be a short monastery loop: Thiksey for morning prayers, Shey and Stok on the way back. Keep the driving under three hours.

Only then plan Nubra and Pangong, with a night in between at a lower altitude if possible. Carry your own water, layers for the morning, and cash — ATMs are unreliable outside Leh.

The mountains are not going anywhere. The itinerary can wait a day.','/images/ladakh.jpg',15,'field-notes',4,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('street-food-delhi-varanasi','A street-food route from Delhi to Varanasi','Chaat, kachori and kebabs: what to eat, when to eat it, and how to stay well.','Indian street food is one of the great pleasures of travelling here, and the rules for enjoying it are simple: eat where the queue is local, eat it hot, and drink bottled or filtered water.

In Delhi, start with a chaat plate in the old city in the late afternoon. The stalls that turn over fastest are the safest and usually the best. A kebab dinner in Jamia Nagar or Nizamuddin is worth the detour.

In Varanasi, breakfast is kachori with sabzi, eaten standing at a counter near the ghats. In winter, look for malaiyo, a saffron milk foam that only appears in the cold months.

Carry a small bottle of hand sanitiser, skip cut fruit from carts, and give your stomach a quiet day after a long train. It is not a failure to eat dal and rice for dinner.

The best food on this route is not in restaurants. Ask your host where they eat.','/images/food.jpg',13,'food',5,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial'),
('monsoon-hill-stations','Hill stations in the monsoon','Munnar, Coorg and Shillong in the rain: what closes, what opens, and why it is worth it.','The monsoon is the most beautiful and least predictable time to be in the Western Ghats and the northeast. Waterfalls run full, the tea is bright green, and the crowds disappear.

Munnar in July is mist and rain, with occasional clear mornings. Eravikulam stays open but the high ridge is often clouded; plan the viewpoint for the first clear morning rather than a fixed day.

Coorg''s estate homestays are at their best in the rain — fireplaces, long lunches, and the smell of wet coffee. Waterfalls such as Abbey are spectacular, but the steps are slippery and swimming is unsafe.

Shillong and Meghalaya get the heaviest rain in the country. Roads to the root bridges can close, so build in a spare day and check with locals before setting out.

Pack quick-dry layers, a proper rain shell and shoes with grip. Accept that the plan will change, and keep the itinerary loose.','/images/coorg.jpg',7,'field-notes',6,'TRIPSPHERE Editorial');
